00:32:49,793 --> 00:32:55,590
I suddenly see for what we're doing a real need for everybody that wants to deploy AI at the frontier,

395
00:32:55,640 --> 00:33:00,196
they have to have some solution to running these models orders of magnitude faster.

396
00:33:00,246 --> 00:33:08,426
Um you know, for the frontier labs, there's this window of kind of premium capability, premium intelligence that is their entire raison d'être.

397
00:33:08,476 --> 00:33:11,790
You know, otherwise we'd all be using Kimmy models all the time.

398
00:33:11,840 --> 00:33:15,670
Um and so as you do have this pressure from behind from open source, you know,

399
00:33:15,720 --> 00:33:22,663
it does become clearly even more important for folks who want to deploy at the frontier to have every aspect of what that means.

400
00:33:22,713 --> 00:33:29,062
So, the best model weights, but also then the fastest deployment, so you can do the most reasoning in the in the shortest amount of time.

401
00:33:29,112 --> 00:33:29,516
And so,

402
00:33:29,566 --> 00:33:35,160
I think this kind of premium end of of of the chip space where you're optimizing for

403
00:33:35,210 --> 00:33:39,864
speed over everything else becomes a kind of very important part of this uh part of this world.

404
00:33:39,914 --> 00:33:46,803
I think one thing that is notable about, you know, the dynamic between a chip vendor and a frontier lab,

405
00:33:46,853 --> 00:33:51,858
you know, if you look at Fractile, we try to be as aligned as possible with the needs of the frontier.

406
00:33:51,908 --> 00:33:54,390
We try to look a bit like some of those teams as well.

407
00:33:54,440 --> 00:33:57,834
We have people that really think deeply about these workloads and so on.

408
00:33:57,884 --> 00:34:02,710
So, one of the cases that I think we sometimes need to make is explaining why we believe that over,

409
00:34:02,760 --> 00:34:09,003
you know, multiple decades the world sustains some kind of frontier third-party chip players.

410
00:34:09,053 --> 00:34:12,710
You know, why does this not just roll up into these into these labs?

411
00:34:12,760 --> 00:34:20,070
Um and I think that comes down to the kind of asymmetric game that all of these labs and frontier players have to play,

412
00:34:20,120 --> 00:34:26,599
uh where they're exposed to an enormous risk if they go all in on a hardware bet.

413
00:34:26,649 --> 00:34:29,824
Um Suppose I'm, you know, I'm lab one,

414
00:34:29,874 --> 00:34:36,882
uh and I've gone all in on some proprietary silicon and then lab two discovers some new computational breakthrough that delivers

415
00:34:36,932 --> 00:34:43,641
far better computational efficiencies for the same level of intelligence but it only works on the chip that they've decided to deploy.

416
00:34:43,691 --> 00:34:51,113
I could die in the nine months before I get to deploy enough of that chip that I've also now get into that kind of 5x in computational efficiency.

417
00:34:51,163 --> 00:34:58,770
And so there is a really urgent need for these chips to actually deploy the same these companies to deploy the same platforms as one another.

418
00:34:58,820 --> 00:35:03,550
They're playing different games so right they're trying to now compete you know in the model layer.

419
00:35:03,600 --> 00:35:10,444
I think having deeply differentiated bets and going all in on those differentiated bets at the chip layer

420
00:35:10,494 --> 00:35:14,710
is sort of an irrational and a very dangerous move for anybody that is playing at the frontier.

421
00:35:14,760 --> 00:35:16,099
Well, I think that's a great place to end.

